<article class="content editorial-guide">
 <h1>Editorial standards and accuracy</h1>
 <p>Sunnah Tools is an independent collection of free Islamic calculators and practical planners. We aim to show both what a tool calculates and what it cannot decide. The information is educational: it is not a fatwa, medical advice or legal advice, and the site does not claim endorsement from a particular scholar or institution.</p>
 <h2>Sources you can check</h2>
 <p>Our guides link to Qur'an verses, identifiable hadith reports, official guidance and technical calculation resources when relevant. A hadith citation does not by itself settle questions of grading, interpretation or jurisprudence. We distinguish textual references from scholarly views, estimated calculations and clinical or legal evidence.</p>
 <h2>Different religious opinions</h2>
 <p>Fiqh questions may differ between the Hanbali, Hanafi, Shafi'i and Maliki schools and between local religious authorities. A calculator cannot choose a binding ruling for someone's circumstances. Where a distinction affects a result, we explain the assumption and encourage users to check their trusted qualified authority.</p>
 <h2>How the calculations work</h2>
 <p>Prayer-time tools use the open-source <a href="https://github.com/batoulapps/adhan-js" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">adhan-js</a> astronomy library with configurable calculation methods. We distinguish prayer starts from mosque congregation times. Read our <a href="/guides/prayer-time-methods/">prayer-time methodology guide</a>.</p>
 <p>Islamic date tools may use a calculated calendar, including Umm al-Qura, rather than verified local moon sightings. Dates and religious observances can vary by community. Read our <a href="/guides/hijri-calendar-and-moon-sighting/">Hijri calendar guide</a>.</p>
 <p>Money tools show estimates based on the assets, rates and assumptions supplied by visitors; they do not independently establish a Zakat obligation, personal eligibility or a legally valid will. Read the <a href="/guides/zakat-nisab-and-fitrana/">Zakat and Nisab guide</a>. Health-related tools, including Hijama, distinguish traditional references from scientific evidence and professional treatment advice.</p>
 <h2>Testing and corrections</h2>
 <p>The <a href="https://github.com/omairarshad/sunnah-tools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">source repository</a> contains the website and automated calculation and generated-page checks. Tests can help find technical mistakes, but they cannot certify the accuracy of every religious, medical or legal conclusion.</p>
 <p>Our <a href="/contact/">contact and corrections page</a> explains how to report a broken tool, a questionable reference or an accessibility issue. We review reports against their sources and can correct the website; there is no promise of a specific response time.</p>
 <h2>Advertising and independence</h2>
 <p>Tools are free. The homepage also links to separately operated projects. Advertising is disabled while AdSense approval is pending. If advertising starts, advertisements will be labelled and will not be presented as religious endorsements.</p>
 <p><small>Published 16 November 2026. These standards describe the site as currently maintained; they do not imply a formal scholarly review board.</small></p>
